**Why I did this:** I wanted to run Python from any terminal window.
 
**What it accomplishes:** Ticking the PATH box tells Windows where Python is installed. PATH is a list of folders Windows searches whenever you type a command.
 
**How it works:** Because Python's folder is on that list, I can simply type either of these from any Command Prompt or terminal without browsing to Python's installation folder:
 
```text
python
```
 
```text
python --version
```
 
> [!WARNING]
> Forgetting to tick "Add python.exe to PATH" is one of the easiest mistakes beginners make. If it is left unchecked, Windows has no way of knowing where Python lives, and typing the commands above usually gives an error like this:
>
> ```text
> 'python' is not recognized as an internal or external command.
> ```
>
> Fixing it later often means manually editing Windows Environment Variables or reinstalling Python entirely.
 
**Why it matters in production:** Support technicians and administrators run scripts from the command line all the time. A missing PATH entry is a very common cause of "it works on my machine but not on yours" tickets.
 
By ticking the checkbox I avoided the problem completely. It only takes a second, but it is arguably the most important option in the whole installation.
 
### Step 3: Verify the Installation
 
I wanted to confirm that Windows recognised the installation. I opened **Command Prompt** by searching for `cmd` in the Windows Start menu and ran:
 
```text
python --version
```
 
Windows displayed the installed version. The output looked similar to:
 
```text
Python 3.x.x
```
 
